          <h2>The benefit categories behind WellFinder treatments</h2>
          <p>Sun Life group plans place most wellness care under paramedical services. A paramedical claim pays toward visits with a registered practitioner, such as a physiotherapist or a registered massage therapist, up to the limits your plan sets. The categories that matter most for WellFinder readers are:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Physiotherapy, which covers pelvic floor physiotherapy, vestibular rehabilitation and shockwave therapy, along with clinical Pilates when a physiotherapist leads the session</li>
            <li>Chiropractic, which some clinical Pilates and golf assessment clinics bill under</li>
            <li>Massage therapy, which covers TMJ massage, lymphatic drainage and head spa treatments given by a registered massage therapist</li>
            <li>Naturopathy, which covers microneedling, IV and IM vitamin therapy and fertility consultations with a naturopathic doctor</li>
            <li>Acupuncture, which covers fertility acupuncture, cosmetic acupuncture and facial gua sha from a registered acupuncturist</li>
            <li>Psychology and psychotherapy, which cover couples therapy, sleep coaching and mental performance coaching</li>
            <li>Speech therapy, which covers singing voice therapy, accent modification and stuttering therapy</li>
            <li>Dietitian services, which cover sports nutrition consultations with a registered dietitian</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Your plan may include some of these categories and leave out others. Annual maximums are common, and many plans cap the amount paid for each visit or apply the reasonable and customary limits Sun Life sets for each service. The practitioner also needs to be registered or licensed in your province, and your receipt has to show that registration.</p>

          <h2>How claiming works with Sun Life</h2>
          <p>Most members claim in the my Sun Life mobile app or on mysunlife.ca. You sign in, choose the claim type, enter the provider and the date of service, and add a photo of your receipt. Sun Life says most claims are paid within 48 hours when direct deposit is set up, and the app shows your claim status and your remaining balances as you go. A few habits make claims smoother:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Keep the receipt from every visit, with the practitioner name, registration number, date and amount paid</li>
            <li>Submit the claim soon after the visit, while the details are fresh</li>
            <li>Check the claim in the app afterwards to see what was paid and what remains under your maximum</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Some clinics can send the claim to Sun Life for you at the appointment. Sun Life members can search for providers on Lumino Health, the Sun Life provider network, which lists practitioners with ratings from other members. Direct billing still follows your plan rules, and you pay any part the plan does not cover before you leave the clinic.</p>
          <p>Keep your receipts after you claim. Sun Life can ask to see them later, and employer guides for members commonly advise holding them for at least 12 months.</p>

          <h2>Health and personal spending accounts</h2>
          <p>Many Sun Life plans add one or both of two accounts. A Health Spending Account, or HSA, holds credits from your employer that you can claim for eligible medical expenses, following the list the Canada Revenue Agency allows. Members often use an HSA for the part of a visit the main plan did not pay, or for an eligible service their plan does not list at all.</p>
          <p>A Personal Spending Account, or PSA, is aimed at wellness costs such as fitness classes, sports fees and other items your employer chooses to allow. Money paid from a PSA is usually a taxable benefit. You can see both balances in the my Sun Life app, and you claim against them in the same claim flow as a regular health claim. Amounts, carry forward rules and eligible items are set by your employer and vary widely.</p>

